<p>Australian counter-terrorism police charged three women who returned from Syria with children on Thursday. Two were arrested at Melbourne airport on slavery-related crimes against humanity allegations, and a third was arrested at Sydney airport on terrorism allegations. The returning group comprised four women and nine children, all Australian citizens, after years in detention camps in Syria following the collapse of Islamic State’s territorial control. Police said the two women arrested in Melbourne, a 53-year-old mother and her 31-year-old daughter, allegedly held a female slave in Syria after travelling there in 2014 to support Islamic State. The older woman faces allegations including possessing, using and trading a slave, while the younger woman faces two slavery charges. Janai Safar, 32, appeared in a New South Wales bail court on Friday after police charged her with entering or remaining in a declared conflict zone and joining a terrorist organisation; the Guardian reported that she was refused bail.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/LwdKtXElp4hs49qhVEDQEEcjdKRelUNNNUSmEP7QIs3yhX-bOZbPvv-wtKZIuz69pEZP3bwCSEOvSL2ZAUoV9lKslxY9yIjemZgaJn3eDc7G' alt='A general view of the Roj camp near Derik, Syria, April 24, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Alleged purchase: Australian Federal Police alleged the older Melbourne defendant acted as an accessory to the purchase of a slave for $10,000.</li><li>Possible sentences: Reuters reported that the slavery-related crimes against humanity charges carry a maximum penalty of 25 years in prison, while Safar’s alleged terrorism offences carry maximum penalties of 10 years.</li><li>Fourth returnee: A fourth woman who travelled with the group had not been arrested, according to accounts citing Australian police.</li><li>Camp location: The Citizen identified the Syrian detention site where the women had been held as Roj camp.</li></ul><blockquote>All I can say is the safety of the community is the number one priority for all agencies involved, - Stephen Nutt</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Australian Federal Police: Stephen Nutt said the case remained an active investigation into very serious allegations and that police would seek to bring alleged offenders before the courts. (<a href='https://444.hu/2026/05/08/ket-ausztral-not-is-emberiesseg-elleni-buncselekmenyekkel-vadolnak'>444.hu</a>)</li><li>Janai Safar&#x27;s lawyer: Safar&#x27;s lawyer raised concerns in court that custody would cut off contact with one of her family members before bail was refused. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/may/08/is-linked-mother-daughter-charged-crimes-against-humanity-court-ntwnfb'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Australian political opposition: The Coalition accused the Labor government of having “lost control of the borders” after the women returned to Australia. (<a href='https://www.news.com.au/national/politics/labor-vows-to-keep-australians-safe-as-coalition-declares-isis-brides-national-security-scandal/news-story/d6f84d2987fc7769ab1f50bc859fbace'>news.com.au</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/05/linked-australian-women-charged-keeping-slave-syria'>IS-linked Australian women charged with keeping slave in Syria</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/two-australian-women-linked-to-isis-charged-with-keeping-slave-in-syria-11465001#publisher=newsstand'>Two Australian Women Linked To ISIS Charged With Keeping Slave In Syria</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/australasia/article/3352825/islamic-state-linked-australian-women-charged-keeping-slave-syria'>Islamic State-linked Australian women charged with keeping slave in Syria</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/may/08/woman-charged-with-joining-islamic-state-syria-sydney-royal-commission-antisemitism-hearings-anthony-albanese-labor-angus-taylor-coaliti